+---
+description: "Browser tab type for the right Sidebar: an address bar over a sandboxed web preview, and the surface every workspace HTML file opens in."
+kind: "package-reference"
+---
+
+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-browser
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+A `browser` tab type for the right Sidebar. It opens two ways. The guide offers it as a page — one address bar over one sandboxed `<iframe>`, where bare loopback addresses take `http` and everything else without a scheme takes `https`. And it claims every `.html` / `.htm` file address in the workspace, so opening one from the file tree, a chat link, or the produced-files row lands here rather than in the document preview.
+
+That second claim is what makes the browser the default way to look at a workspace page. The document preview's builtin HTML renderer packs a document into a blob URL, which reads a page but cannot serve it: no origin, no relative fetches, no navigation. A browser tab instead frames a capability URL minted by [`@deepseek-ai/dsh-host-preview-static`](../../host/preview-static/README.md), so the page loads its own stylesheets, scripts, and images the way a browser would.
+
+The bar's text, the shown URL, the reload counter, and the file binding live in a Session-scoped Slot store bucketed by tab id, so pane moves and remounts lose nothing and two browser tabs navigate independently.
+
+## What it registers
+
+- **The type** — `ctx.sidebarRightTabs.register(...)` with id `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-browser`, kind `browser`, band `builtin`, the pattern `dsh-resource://file/**` narrowed by a `canOpen` that accepts only HTML, and one guide entry ordered after the file tree's.
+- **The body** — the keyed `sidebar.right.pane.tab` seat under the type's id. A 38px address bar in the pane-header style of the file tree and document preview, then the frame or the empty state. Reload remounts a page tab's frame or re-mints a file tab's; the tab record's abort signal forgets the bucket.
+
+### Why the band and `canOpen` both matter
+
+`text` claims the same `dsh-resource://file/**` pattern at `fallback` — it is the plain viewer anything more specific should beat — so a `builtin` claim takes every HTML file. `canOpen` is the other half: without it the browser would outrank the document preview for Markdown, images, and PDFs too, because the band alone cannot tell them apart. The type in force is chosen once per address; there is no per-file "open with" menu.
+
+### The two modes, and the two sandboxes
+
+A **page** tab frames whatever the user typed, under the wide sandbox an ordinary page needs (`allow-scripts allow-same-origin allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-downloads`) — visiting it is the user's own choice.
+
+A **file** tab — recognised by `tab.contentId` parsing as a file address — frames a capability URL instead, under `sandbox="allow-scripts"` with **no** `allow-same-origin`, so the served document runs in an opaque origin and cannot reach the app embedding it. A small bridge the host appends to served HTML reports the frame's location upward, which is how the bar follows in-page navigation. That mode reads no bytes of its own: the host serves them.
+
+The binding is where a file tab **starts**, not a cage. Its bar answers to both kinds of input, and `resolvesInWorkspace` draws the line:
+
+| Typed | Read as | Effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| `other.html`, `pages/index.html`, `../sibling.htm` | a workspace page | re-mints a capability inside the workspace |
+| `https://example.com`, `localhost:5173`, `example.com/page.html` | an address | navigates the tab out; the file binding is cleared and the tab is an ordinary page tab |
+
+The sandbox follows whatever is actually framed, so a tab that started on a workspace file gets the ordinary sandbox back the moment it leaves — an external site framed under `allow-scripts` alone would break on its own storage APIs.
+
+### Where the file path comes from
+
+The address bar shows and navigates a **workspace-relative** path, but this package never decides which workspace owns the file. A `dsh-resource://file/session/…` address carries a path relative to the *Session's* cwd whenever the file sits under it, and a Session's cwd may be a subdirectory that equals no registered workspace root — so the browser half only rebuilds the absolute path from the Session cwd it holds, and the host plugin picks the containing workspace and reports the relative path back. See the host package's README for the resolution rules.
+
+## Model Experience
+
+None, as the package is a browser-side viewer that registers no tool, prompt section, or session event.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+None; what the user reads here never enters a model request.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **Pages can refuse framing.** Sites sending `X-Frame-Options` or a `frame-ancestors` CSP render blank; the body offers no fallback beyond opening the address elsewhere.
+- **No navigation stack.** Back/forward inside the frame belongs to the page itself; the bar does not track in-frame navigation, so it can drift from the frame's real location.
+- **Taking HTML from `text` is not reversible in the UI.** Every `.html` opens in the browser; the document preview's own HTML renderer is still registered but nothing routes to it, and the tab-type registry has no per-file "open with" menu.
+- **A file outside every registered workspace is refused.** The host answers `outside-workspace` and the bar shows why, rather than serving a file no workspace owns.
+- **A workspace directory whose name carries a dot must be clicked, not typed.** `v1.2/index.html` reads as the host `v1.2`, because a dotted first segment is how any browser reads a URL. The file tree reaches it normally.
+- **A relative path that is not HTML is read as an address.** `notes.md` becomes `https://notes.md`, exactly as a browser bar would treat it; non-HTML files are opened from the file tree instead.
+
+**Runtime invariant:** No companion is published. The type declaration, one dictionary effect, and one keyed body registration live exactly as long as this plugin; per-tab state lives only in the declared Slot store.
